<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="glycerol degradation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-4261" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Bacteria&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: GLYCEROL Glycerol| uptake in bacteria is mediated by the glycerol diffusion facilitator, an integral membrane protein catalyzing the rapid equilibration of concentration gradients of glycerol across the cytoplasmic membrane |CITS: [8436953]|. Intracellular glycerol is converted to |FRAME: GLYCEROL-3P| by the enzyme glycerol kinase that uses ATP as phosphoryl donor. |FRAME: GLYCEROL-3P &quot;Glycerol-3-P&quot;| is not a substrate of the glycerol diffusion facilitator and thus remains in the cell, where it is further metabolized. As a result, the driving force for the uptake of glycerol is generated by the phosphorylation of glycerol by glycerol kinase |CITS: [9162046]|. While |FRAME: GLYCEROL-3P| can not leave the cytoplasm, it can be imported into the cell by the GlpT transporter, a member of the major facilitator superfamily, that couples the import of glycerol-3-P into the cytoplasm to the export of inorganic phosphates from the cytoplasm to the periplasm |CITS: [3522583][12893936]|. In |FRAME:TAX-562| |FRAME: GLYCEROL-3P| can be further metabolized to |FRAME: DIHYDROXY-ACETONE-PHOSPHATE| by either of two membrane-bound enzymes, depending on the growth conditions. The presumed role of this process is the salvage of glycerol and glycerol phosphates generated by the breakdown of phospholipids and triacylglycerol. Under aerobic conditions, a homodimeric aerobic |FRAME: AERGLYC3PDEHYDROG-CPLX &quot;glycerol-3-P dehydrogenase&quot;| (encoded by the |FRAME: EG10394| gene) is produced, which can accept either oxygen or nitrate as the electron acceptor |CITS: [340460]|. Under anaerobic conditions, a different |FRAME: ANGLYC3PDEHYDROG-CPLX &quot;glycerol-3-P dehydrogenase&quot;| is preferentially expressed. This tri-heteromeric protein complex, which is encoded by the &lt;i&gt;glpACB&lt;/i&gt; operon, channels the electrons from |FRAME: GLYCEROL-3P| to either fumarate or nitrate |CITS: [3286606]|. &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Yeast&lt;/b&gt; Under aerobic conditions |FRAME: TAX-4932| is able to utilize glycerol as a sole carbon and energy source. Glycerol degradation is a two-step process that involves translocation. The first step, glycerol phosphorylation, occurs in the cytosol. The product, glycerol-3-phosphate, enters the mitochondrion where it is converted to |FRAME: DIHYDROXY-ACETONE-PHOSPHATE| |CITS:[191434]|. |FRAME: DIHYDROXY-ACETONE-PHOSPHATE| is then returned to the cytosol, where it enters into either glycolysis or gluconeogenesis. The genes encoding the enzymes catalyzing glycerol catabolism are regulated by the carbon source. Gene expression is repressed in the presence of fermentable carbon sources such as glucose, and upregulated by non-fermentable carbon sources such as glycerol or ethanol |CITS:[191434][10536147][11142398]|. &lt;b&gt;This Pathway in Plants&lt;/b&gt; In higher plants, the primary seed storage reserve is triacylglycerol. During seed germination, triacylglycerol is broken down to fatty acids and glycerol. Fatty acids are converted to sucrose via glyoxylate cycle to support seedling growth. Radio tracer experiments have also demonstrated that glycerol can be converted to sucrose as well. The contribution of carbon stored as glycerol is relatively small, being estimated of 5% of the total carbon. Two possible routes of glycerol catabolism have been proposed. In the first pathway, glycerol is phosphorylated to glycerol-3-phosphate by glycerol kinase, and then converted to dihydroxyacetone phosphate by glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. In the second pathway, glycerol is coverted to dihydroxyacetone by NAD-dependent glycerol dehydrogenase, and then phosphorylated to dihydroxyacetone phosphate by dihydroxyacetone kinase. The assertion of the first pathway, not the second pathway, in plants is supported by the following evidence: glycerol kinase and glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase activities are detected in germinating seeds, whereas NAD-dependent glycerol dehydrogenase activity is not; the highest expression of the glycerol kinase and the FAD-dependent glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase genes are detected during seed germination; the glycerol kinase mutant, gli1, of Arabidopsis is disrupted in glycerol catabolism and accumulates glycerol. It is worth noting that, in addition to the mitochondrial FAD-dependent glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, plants also have cytosolic and plastidial NAD-dependent glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase that can convert glycerol-3-phosphate to dihydroxyacetone phosphate. But the NAD-dependent glycerol-3-phosphate dehydrogenase is unlikely to be involved in glycerol degradation since the equilibrium constant favors the synthesis of glycerol-3-phosphate. The product of glycerol degradation, dihydroxyacetone phosphate, can be converted to sugars via gluconeogenesis.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
